{}	base	set08-DEM02_DEME02-1	Yes	Exchange in the widest sense took the form of (1) personal and group trade, (2) and participation in ceremonies and the exchange of ritual paraphernalia AND the spirits with which they were associated. (1) Personal trade was been individual men linked totemically, who regarded each other as ‘kin’ (e.g. brothers) and used the same kinship terms they used for members of their own groups. Such men frequently visited each other’s settlements for periods of up to several weeks. Group trade took place between women in groups at market sites owned by the Kwoma scattered around the edges of the Washkuk Hills. (2) Up until the 1980s, when they ceased being performed, men from both groups regularly participated in ceremonies organized by the other language group. See the answers to questionnaire 1 [DTR]	\N	61	61	5	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
