{}	base	set06b-DEM33	The [q2o2answer] language	I'm inferring. Reasoning: \r\n1)    Tuscan Italian was likely the expected koine used in the time frame when most mixed marriages happened (approx. 1500–1800). 2\r\n2)    Those Maltese who had most opportunities for out-group marriage lived in urban harbour cities and had daily contacts with foreigners and were not completely monolinguals. (Brincat 2021: 204–205.) \r\n3)    Maltese had become familiar with Sicilian before Tuscan Italian, and spoke Tuscan with Sicilian features (Brincat 2021: 205).\r\nErgo, Sicilian (or Tuscan Italian with very Sicilianate features) would have been the common language for both participants.\r\nOther considerations: Both Maltese and Sicilians were Christians, and marriage is a sacrament, so Latin may have been a relevant language in marriage ceremonies. However, Brincat mentions Maltese, Sicilian and, Tuscan Italian being used alongside with Latin in religious contexts (cf. e.g. Brincat 2021: 193)	\N	1238	1238	209	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
