{}	base	set08-DEM96	See answers to previous questions. From the beginning of the nineteenth century Kwoma have actively interacted for the purposes of exchange (1) with the neighbouring Kaunga (Ndu-speakrs) to the north and north-east in the contexts of trade, ceremony (up to the 1980s), and intermarriage (on-going); (2) with the Ndu-speaking Ngala to the south between around 1820 and 1860 in the contexts of ritual and intermarriage; (3) with the Mayo (Tama family) to the west on an on-going basis in the context of ritual and probably intermarriage; and (4) with the Iatmul-speaking Brugnowi (Ndu family) to the south since the 1920s in the context of group trade at market sites midway between their territories. Interaction in marriage and ceremony was also common from the earliest times (c. 1800) to 1950 with the Apukili to the north, but their language has not been identified. It was probably Kwanga, which belongs to the same - Nukuma - family as Kwoma.	\N	\N	1623	1623	\N	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
