{}	base	set06b-DKN05	At places which belong to the [q2o2answer]	Religion and education were heavily intertwined, and most Christian churches "belonged" to the Sicilians (as in that they were established and administered by them, the Maltese were not Christians before this) (Brincat 2021: 75–76, 193). Besides the clergy, other educated individuals like notaries may have tutored pupils, but these people were probably also Sicilians. Additionally, Maltese travelled to Sicily for higher education (Brincat 2021: 76, 194, 364, Said-Zammit 2016: 48). 'At places which belong to some other group' could also be a good choice, especially for the period after the arrival of the Order of St John (cf. Brincat 2021: 203)	\N	3329	3260	552	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
