{}	base	set06b-DLB03_DLB03-1	Yes	Yes' for profession/class (cf. e.g. Brincat 2021: 204) and 'place identity', as cities were the main hubs of contact in labour – not only in the sense that there were more opportunities for work, but there was a great need for workers when the cities were built in the first place (Brincat 2021: 217; Said-Zammit 2016: 78). Upper and middle class women were not expected to work outside the home, but many lower class women had no choice, so sex/gender is not the main factor, but rather class (Said-Zammit 2016: 47, 64; also mentions small-scale textile industry mostly run by peasant women, ibid.: 51, additionally Blouet (1967: 130) mentions women and children working in cotton industry).	\N	4144	4032	716	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
