{}	base	set06b-DLB25	The [q2o2answer] language	1) The Maltese who had most opportunities for working with Sicilians lived in urban harbour towns. Other groups, in addition to Sicilians, were present in the context of labour (see my comment to DLB01). These Maltese people had daily contacts with foreigners from different countries and were not completely monolinguals. \r\n2) Tuscan Italian was likely the expected koine used in labour (and other domains, too) since the harbor cities were multicultural and multilingual (cf. Brincat 2021: 204–205). \r\n3) Maltese had become familiar with Sicilian before Tuscan Italian and spoke Tuscan with Sicilian features (Brincat 2021: 205). \r\nErgo, when working in multilingual settings, Maltese most likely would have used some sort of Tuscan (with code-switching maybe, depending on their level of fluency; see also my comment to DLB06) but I assume that when speaking with Sicilians specifically, both would have used Sicilian, since the Tuscan Italian that the Maltese spoke was already heavily influenced by Sicilian features.	\N	4934	4822	845	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
