{}	base	set06b-DLC02	Neighbourhood or village	I believe that 'neighbourhood or village' best represents the timeframe. In the rural areas, parishes were local focal points to smaller hamlets and villages (Said-Zammit 2016: 44, 55), while in the urban cities, neighbourhoods might have been the equivalent arena to daily interactions. Urban areas were somewhat segregated according to social class (cf. Said-Zammit 2016: 46, 49). In our timeframe, the importance of cities grows from 1500 onwards; in 1530 only 0,5% of the population lived in cities, by 1797 some 40% did. For example, Valletta had a population of 3400 in 1590, and 20,100 in 1797. (Brincat 2021: 219.) However, there is areal variation. It seems that especially those Maltese who had migrated to cities from rural areas modified their speech, suppressing distinct dialectal features and adapting the urban vernacular (Brincat 2021: 232–233). So, it is likely that Maltese spoke in an unmonitored way in their villages of origin, but not in the in the cities/larger urban centres.	\N	5335	5178	905	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
