{}	base	set16-DLC04	The [q2o2answer] language	Here, we have to make a distinction between the historical and present-day behaviour. Until only one or at best two generations ago, the Chipaya used Aymara when getting into contact with Aymara speakers and especially men were usually bilingual in Chipaya and Aymara. Thus, my choice here reflects a rather historical context. Today, Spanish is largely replacing Aymara and hence, the language used in contact situations is typically Andean Spanish. However, Chipaya men above the age of 50 tend to be trilingual in Chipaya, Aymara and Spanish, whereas older Chipaya men (say roughly above 70) are rather bilingual in Chipaya and Aymara. The younger generation is bilingual in Chipaya and Spanish.	\N	5485	5328	922	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
