{}	base	set06b-DLC25_DLC25-2	No	I'm uncertain about this, since everyday activities are rarely described in the literature. The society was highly segmented by class (Said-Zammit 2016: 45–46, 66–67), so I believe that the 'local community' was different to people of different classes. However, I don't think a person's background affected the involvement in local community, as long as it happened in their "respective" local community. As noted in D3 responses to other domains, a woman's duty was to take care of children and the house, but at the same time many women, especially of lower classes, had to work outside the house to earn a living (Said-Zammit 2016: 63–64), which I believe also would have led to contacts in the community in general.	\N	6011	5854	1013	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
