{}	base	set06b-DTR02_DTR02-6	Yes	Maltese traded at least cotton (Borg 2012: 1035; Cassar 2002: 13, Said-Zammit 2016: 51, 71, Blouet 1967: 130–132) and meat products (Said-Zammit 2016: 51) to Sicily. Sicilian wheat (Blouet 1967: 144; Brincat 2021: 131; Borg 2012: 1035; Cassar 2002: 6–9, 19; Mack Smith 1968: 136–137, Said-Zammit 2016: 62) and wine (Zaid-Sammit 2016: 51) were imported to Malta. Brincat (2021: 212, 219) describes the Maltese society pre-1500 as being mostly focused on agriculture and fishing. Skilled craftsmen arrived from Sicily to Malta, Brincat (2021: 238) also mentions the passing on of new techniques, along with the terminology, from immigrant craftsmen to apprentices/assistant. Cassar (2002: 11) describes Malta as a "society which depended heavily on transactions of low value and bulky commodities". Based on this, I doubt that the Maltese traded luxury items or manufactured goods to Sicilians. Various documents (receipts, taxation) imply that manufactured and luxury goods (Brincat 2021: 77 mentions e.g. cartj 'paper', Jnchensu 'incence') were sold in Malta, and as the names of these objects are in Sicilian, I'm suspecting that Maltese acquired these from Sicilians. Sicily also produced silk and sugar cane (Mack Smith 1968: 58), and exported e.g. sulphur, saltpetre, ostrich feathers (ibid.: 91. NB! Not necessarily traded to Malta), and other European products made their way to Malta mainly through Sicilian ports (Blouet 1967: 135).	\N	6816	6608	1101	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
