{}	base	set13-DTR0b	Definitely, pre-colonial, i.e., before 1938, but with the advent of mining in 1990 more Hewa now come to the Ipili area. With colonialism, the Ipili and Hewa both had violent encounters with Australian colonial patrols. For the Ipili, colonialism brought road building in the higher altitudes of the valley which drew them away from the Lagaip River (the boundary between the Ipili and Hewa). As well, climate change has shifted the altitude at which Anopheles mosquitos are found, and the Ipili have moved upward to avoid malaria. So while it seems that there was more interaction in the past, especially pre-1938 and before, the mining situation has brought more Hewa, but not really for trade, it seems more from Hewa curiosity.	Definitely, pre-colonial, i.e., before 1938, but with the advent of mining in 1990 more Hewa now come to the Ipili area. With colonialism, the Ipili and Hewa both had violent encounters with Australian colonial patrols. For the Ipili, colonialism brought road building in the higher altitudes of the valley which drew them away from the Lagaip River (the boundary between the Ipili and Hewa). As well, climate change has shifted the altitude at which Anopheles mosquitos are found, and the Ipili have moved upward to avoid malaria. So while it seems that there was more interaction in the past, especially pre-1938 and before, the mining situation has brought more Hewa, but not really for trade, it seems more from Hewa curiosity.	\N	7270	7062	\N	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
