{}	base	set06b-DTR28	Yes	Contacts in the domain of trade were the most intense in harbour areas, although it was common that people travelled from the rural areas to trade, even daily (Brincat 2021: 235, Borg 2012: 1037). Historical documents mention harbour or city speech, which seems to refer to code-mixing/switching: a document from 1557 mentions this ("...homines et donni mischiano lo vulgare italico con lo Maltese", quoted in Brincat 2021: 220), later in 1796 Vassalli described the "most corrupt" city dialect: "[Maltese] barbarize the native language with Sicilian, Italian, French and other expressions" (quoted in Brincat 2021: 229). Brincat hypothesizes that some sort of pidginized Maltese-Romance variety would have been used already mid-13th century (ibid.: 70). Borg (2012: 1037) mentions that Maltese speakers tried to suppress certain features "particularly marked with respect to the foreigner's speech" out of social pressure.	\N	7759	7551	1229	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
