{}	base	set32-DTR36	Somewhat proximate to each other	As noted in other sections, distance varies throughout the extents of Yurok and Karuk land. Buckle (1982) furthermore notes that trade sometimes involved geographically proximal communities, and sometimes involved individuals traveling longer distances to communities that do not speak the same language. On average trade between Yurok and Karuk speakers may have involved somewhat proximate individuals and groups.	\N	7975	7767	1266	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
{}	base	set33-DTR36	Very far from each other	In prehistory, there was known trading between Kodiak (pre-Alutiiq, presumably Aleut) and Lake Iliamna, at least a day's travel if not more; and between lower and upper Kenai (potentially between prehistoric Aleut and Eyak), as well as between Kodiak and lower Kenai.  These were not considered unusual distances.  There was, of course, intercommunity trading between islands on the Aleutian Chain, which may or may not have involved the redistribution of slaves, but I consider that of less importance in language contact effects, because it would have been on a smaller scale, given the decreasing population going westward along the Chain.	\N	7976	7768	1268	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
