{}	base	set05-OD5	B	I don't know and Robinson (2011) doesn't say, though he does make the following comments (p.13-14):\nIn village of Togarao, where I spent the majority of my time during my field research,\nRotokas is the primary language of communication. It is the first language used by children and\nit is the preferred language in day-to-day life... However, the situation is somewhat different in Wakunai Station, a village through which the highway of East Bougainville runs. At Wakunai Station, the same dialect of Rotokas is spoken as the lingua franca but there are many more members of the community who do not speak Rotokas as well as travellers who pass through the area, and Tok Pisin is the primarily language of communication with these individuals. ... Tok Pisin is also used as a lingua franca among the minority of villagers who, due to unusual circumstances, do not speak Rotokas. There are a few individuals who have a passive command of Rotokas and\nare able to understand the language but not to speak it.	\N	8544	8294	1363	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
{}	base	set06b-OD5	Fairly high proportion	As above, I have no demographics on this topic, so I'm basing my response on inference. In our time frame, there were multiple linguistic, cultural, and ethnic groups living in Sicily, including Normans, Lombards, Frenchmen, Catalans, Genoese, Castilian-speaking Spaniards (Mack Smith 1968: 58, 62–63, 103, 120; Trovato & Valenti 2013). Additionally, the island had a significant Greek and (Siculo-)Arabic speaking populations, including a Jewish population (Mack Smith 1968; Trovato & Valenti 2013: 11, 35–47), and an Albanian speaking diaspora arrived at the turn of the 15th century (Trovato 2013). Greeks established colonies in Sicily in 8th c., and Greek remained a significant language even during the time when Sicily was a Roman province and after that (Mack Smith 1968: 3; Trovato & Valenti 2013: 11–21). Additionally, a minority of Maltese also used Sicilian, especially educated individuals (Brincat 2021: passim, but cf. especially pages 77–86 and 109–115) Thus, I've chosen the response 'Fairly high proportion’.	\N	8545	8295	1359	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
