{}	base	set06b-OI7	Neither positive nor negative	As above. The puristic attitudes seem not a contemporary phenomenon: Vassalli, a scholar writing in 1796, described the dialect of the cities as "the most corrupt, not only due to the presence of so many foreigners, which exerts a strong influence on languages, but because of the fanatical way in which certain people give a Maltese form to foreign words and in so doing barbarize the native language with Sicilian, Italian, French, and other expressions, not only by adopting the new names of foreign customs and objects, which is sometimes necessary, but because they adopt them and substitute them for the truly national and expressive ones" (quoted in Brincat 2021: 229), but on the other hand, Vassalli "had a static concept of language and idealized the rustic speech of this home town" (Brincat 2021: 230) so this might now reflect the general attitudes. In general, I've found no references that the attitudes the Maltese had towards linguistic transfer varied depening on the language.	\N	8993	8743	1399	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
