{}	base	set06b-OL2_OL2-7	No	I chose 'yes' for hierarchy groups associated with profession or wealth (such as class and caste). Only a small minority of Maltese went to schools, and according to Said-Zammit (2016: 65) "education was only available for the socially privileged", which I'm inferring to mean both wealth and class. As mentioned above, the peasant population was illiterate. Class, profession, and wealth are often overlapping categories. Lower class people did not have the financial means to educate themselves or their children, nor did they work in professions that required abilities to read and write. Gender may have been relevant, as women were not expected to participate in labour outside the house (Said-Zammit 2016: 47, 64) but I don't think this constitutes a 'yes' given our criteria.	\N	9336	9086	1439	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
