{}	base	set06b-OS2	Four levels	As stated above (CID OS1), Sicily was governed by foreign powers; first the Normans (11–12th c.), Swabians and Angevins (13th c.), then as an independent kingdom by the Aragonese (13–14th c.), and lastly Habsburg Spain (from 1480 until the French took over in 1714) (Mack Smith 1968; Brincat 2021) and Malta was administratively a part of Sicily. Since 1530, Malta was governed by The Order of St. John, while Sicily remained part of the Spanish kingdom. During the Spanish rule, Sicily had a parliament, modelled after the Aragonese custom (Mack Smith 1968: 124–132), and the Viceroy was above the jurisdictional power of the parliament (ibid.: 128–129). The catholic church was also an organization with major jurisdictional power.	\N	9440	9190	1452	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
