{}	base	set06b-OS7	One or more indigenous towns of more than 5,000 inhabitants but none of more than 50,000	I've found no specific figures. In our time frame, the Maltese in general moved to larger settlements: larger rural centers or the urban harbour towns. The population growth was rapid in cities: according to Brincat (2021: 219), in 1530 only 0,5% of the population lived in cities, while by 1797 some 40% did. For example, Valletta had a population of 3400 in 1590, and 20,100 in 1797. Outside the harbor, villages grew too. Blouet (1967: 95) mentions some village populations between ca. 4000–6000 in 1766. Moreover, "In general terms any village with less than 200 inhabitants, at the beginning of the seventeenth century, was subsequently abandoned." (ibid.: 96). Towards the start of our time frame, the mean size must have been smaller, and my response reflects the later part of the time frame, but I only have figures of that time period.	\N	9580	9330	1480	\N	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
