9676	{"comment": "Presumably since at least the year 200 CE (cf. Piha 2021). Indirect sources (Snorre Sturluson's Konungas\\u00f6gur and other Norse sagas) as well as archaeological findings (South Saami items in Scandinavian sites and vice versa) point to regular contact between the South Saami of that time and the Scandinavian people of that time. These two groups must have had some knowledge of each other's languages, even if there is only indirect evidence. One period of dense contact is the pre-Christianization time (pre-1200). Another period of dense contact started during the 20th century, in which the national states Sweden and Norway started their \\"assimilation politics\\" and suppressed the use of the South Saami language.", "start": 1000, "end": 2020}	base	DLC0aNset30	\N	\N	59	371	31	\N	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	2026-09-27 10:36:49.766157+02	t
